# Commit scopes name the module

<!-- core -->
**A commit's scope names the module or area inside the repository that the commit moved** —
`scripts`, `finish-contract`, `spectre` — never the change name, the change's Jira key, or a task
id. A scope is optional: a subject carrying none, `fix: <subject>`, already satisfies this. Where a
commit spans several modules, name the one carrying its substance, or a broader area covering them —
never a list.
<!-- /core -->

## Why

`<type>(<scope>): <subject>` exists so a reader scanning `git log --oneline` sees what part of the
codebase moved without opening the commit. A scope naming the change or a ticket repeats information
the branch and the tracker already carry, and tells the reader nothing about where the change landed.

## The three prohibited shapes

- **The change name** — `feat(kan-239-run-2-asserts-base-branch-and-archives-via-pr): …` names the
  change, not a module.
- **The change's bare Jira key** — `feat(kan-239): …` is the same defect with the descriptive suffix
  trimmed off.
- **A task id** — `fix(3.2): …` names a line in a plan, not a part of the codebase.

Anything else is allowed. There is no vocabulary of legal module names to maintain; the rule refuses
three known-wrong shapes and accepts everything else.

## The guard

Inside the flow pipeline, `<agents repo>/scripts/check-task-commit-fields.sh` — its Go port's `tcfCheckCommitScope`
(`<agents repo>/stats/internal/guard/taskcommitfields.go`) — enforces
this on every task's **declared** `**Commit:**` field, at the point `/flow` commits the task —
so a plan that declares one of the three prohibited shapes fails the guard even when the implementer
reproduces it exactly. Outside the pipeline, this rule is the only enforcement: apply it by hand.

## Examples

- `feat(scripts): add alpha` — a module, passes.
- `fix: drop an invented spec citation` — no scope at all, passes; a scope is optional.
- `feat(kan-900-helpers): add alpha` — contains a Jira key as a substring but is not equal to it,
  passes; the check is equality, never a substring match.
- `feat(kan-202-commit-split-and-module-scopes): …` — the change name, refused.
- `fix(3.2): …` — a dotted task id, refused.

Applies globally, to every commit, not only inside the flow pipeline.
